WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.240
I started becoming aware of who was comfortable being where with me.

2
00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:08.880
There were some people who were very comfortable with me in the cafeteria, they were comfortable with me in the dorm,

3
00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:12.960
but out on campus in the [Student] Union, they’d pass me like they’d never seen me before.

4
00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:19.640
It’s funny, whites often think that the worst thing that can happen to a black is to be yelled a racial slur at. [scoffs] Please,

5
00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:21.500
give me racial slurs!

6
00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:24.700
Just don’t give me a friend who ignores me in public.

7
00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:32.260
A friend who embraced me intimately and then get in public and she’s never seen me before.

8
00:00:32.260 --> 00:00:35.580
Give me racial slurs over that.

9
00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:40.740
And then I met Marie Biondo and her sister . . . These were my friends.

10
00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:43.220
The Biondos had a Jeep that we would go riding in.

11
00:00:43.220 --> 00:00:47.420
A convertible Jeep, which was fun.

12
00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:49.480
So I got to do those kind of wild college things.

13
00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:51.200
It was like, “Oh, this is being in college!”

14
00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:53.300
Riding in a Jeep with the top off of it.

15
00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:56.380
Going to get malts; I had never gone to go get malts.

16
00:00:56.380 --> 00:00:59.940
My mother was always, “I don’t want you hanging around the malt shop.

17
00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:01.900
People are going to say I’m just letting you run wild.”

18
00:01:01.900 --> 00:01:03.420
It was like, “I’m running wild!

19
00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:05.680
I’m having a malt!” [laughs]

20
00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:08.080
Where did you go for malts?

21
00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:12.700
At the pharmacist right off of campus.

22
00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:21.020
It was a Rexall Drug store, it was called Tiger Town Pharmacy.

23
00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:26.640
I remember there seemed to have been a time where there was a question about whether I could sit at the counter.

24
00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:35.360
But I did, but they didn’t know I was black because the Italian girls were dark, too.

25
00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:43.280
We would go in places where they didn’t know I was black, and they would say, “They don’t know you’re black!”

26
00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:45.160
So, a lot of the places I went were like that.

27
00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:46.560
A lot.

28
00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:48.060
How did you feel about that?

29
00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:49.052
Did you think about it at the time?

30
00:01:49.052 --> 00:01:49.252
Yeah.

31
00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:51.600
Yeah I did, yeah.

32
00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:56.880
I knew that in a way I was passing, but I knew that I wasn’t choosing to pass.

33
00:01:56.880 --> 00:01:59.820
I was letting it pass.

34
00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:02.440
I wasn’t saying, “I’m going to go in here and pretend to be white.”

35
00:02:02.440 --> 00:02:06.160
If anyone had asked me, I would have acknowledged being black, absolutely.

36
00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:07.040
I knew that.

37
00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:09.760
But what I wanted was the full college experience,

38
00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:14.760
and if it meant going in those places they went, then I wanted to go in those places.

39
00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:19.600
Because they were college students, not because they were white.

40
00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:22.440
About two years after being there at the university,

41
00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:30.240
after they had a large onslaught of blacks come in and blacks couldn’t go into all those places right off campus,

42
00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:35.560
then the university issued a statement that said, “You will serve all LSU students or you will move your business.”

43
00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:39.800
And then we could go in everywhere.

44
00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:48.060
But I was thrown out of one place when I went with some other blacks to a place I had gone to when they didn’t know.

45
00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:53.740
And I went in that same place, and it was the Bengal . . . No, the Pastime.

46
00:02:53.740 --> 00:03:02.680
They came over immediately and said, “We don’t serve coloreds here.”

47
00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:07.840
Was this part of a deliberate attempt to desegregate these places?

48
00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:08.560
It was.

49
00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:10.080
I knew what I was doing.

50
00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:12.580
I knew that they didn’t accept blacks.
